     1	# vexel-platform-apple
     2	
     3	This crate owns the macOS native window and event boundary. `RgbaFrame` and
     4	`Window::present_rgba` form a pixel presentation seam for owned top-left,
     5	tightly packed RGBA8 data. Input channels are straight (unassociated) sRGB;
     6	alpha is independent coverage. Rows run from visual top to bottom without
     7	padding. The presenter converts each pixel to premultiplied RGBA8 before
     8	declaring `PremultipliedLast` to CoreGraphics. Frame dimensions are limited to
     9	4096 by 4096 (64 MiB), both the exact byte length and allocated `Vec` capacity
    10	are bounded, and backing scale must be one of the window boundary's supported
    11	integral scales: 1, 2, or 3 pixels per point.
    12	
    13	Presentation converts the owned pixels in place, copies them into Core
    14	Foundation data, and fully prepares a CoreGraphics image before changing layer
    15	properties. CoreGraphics interprets the supplied byte rows from top to bottom;
    16	the first row remains the visual top, with no orientation transform. The image
    17	uses the sRGB color space. The provider retains its immutable CFData, and the
    18	layer retains the published CGImage for display. If a guarded native update
    19	fails, the presenter restores the prior contents, scale, and gravity while
    20	retaining the prior image. This is only the frame presentation seam. It does
    21	not integrate a document model, editor workflow, SVG renderer, or complete V1
    22	application flow. Package tests do not establish live AppKit rendering on a
    23	macOS GUI session.
